{{smaller block|That time [for the outward service] to me towards you is Tuesday, and my ''temple'' the Rose in Smithfield.
{{right|''Donne'', Letters, xxiv.}}}}

readings.c3s1:
{{smaller block|The true Christian . . . loves the good, under whatever ''temple'', at whatever altar he may find them.
{{right|''Sydney Smith'', in Lady Holland, iii.}}}}

4. Metaphorically, any place in which the divine presence specially resides.

{{smaller block|Know ye not that your body is the ''temple'' of the Holy Ghost which is in you, which ye have of God, and ye are not your own? {{right|1 Cor. vi. 19.}}}}

{{smaller block|My chamber were no ''temple'', my body were no ''temple'', except God came to it. {{right|''Donne'', Sermons, iv.}}}}

5. [''cap.''] The name of two semi-monastic establishments of the middle ages, one in London, the other in Paris, occupied by the Knights Templars. {{smaller|The Temple Church, London, is the only part of either establishment now existing. On the site of the London Temple the two Inns of Court called the Middle Temple and Inner Temple now stand; they have long been occupied by barristers, and are the joint property of the two societies called the Societies of the Inner and of the Middle Temple, which have the right of calling candidates to the degree of barrister. The Temple in Paris was the prison of Louis XVI. and the royal family during their sufferings in 1792 and 1793.}}

6†. An inn of court.

{{smaller block|A gentle maunciple was ther of a ''temple.''
{{right|''Chaucer'', Gen. Prol. to C. T., l. 567.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Master of the temple.''' See ''master''<sup>1</sup>.—'''Temple jar, temple vase,''' a jar or vase such as are used for the decoration and ceremonial of religious temples in China, Japan, etc.—'''Temple jewelry.''' See ''jewelry.''}}

'''temple'''<sup>1</sup> (tem′pl), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''templed'', ppr. ''templing''. [< ''temple''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.''] To build a temple for; appropriate a temple to; inclose in a temple. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|The heathen (in many places) ''templed'' and adored this drunken god. {{right|''Feltham'', Resolves, i. 84.}}}}

